data.select-the-device-in-the-system#Select t:
The stage uses the portable particle model, whose components Larecs can copy to the device byte by byte.
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle coordinates, using device-compatible scalar arithmetic.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle displacement per unit of simulated time.""" var dx: Float32 var dy: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Time(Copyable, Movable): """Shared simulated step length.""" var delta: Float32 The kernel is the movement kernel from the first simulation, unchanged.
comptime move_filter = Filter().include[Position]().read[Velocity]() comptime move_resources = Resources().read[Time]() def move(context: KernelContext[move_filter, move_resources]): """Moves each row using its velocity and the shared step length. Args: context: Matching rows and read-only time. """ ref time = context.resources.get[Time]() for particle in context: ref position = particle.get[Position]() ref velocity = particle.get[Velocity]() position.x += velocity.dx * time.delta position.y += velocity.dy * time.delta move is a thin kernel, the only kind of kernel device execution accepts. The system chooses the target with the backend parameter of SystemContext.run:
@fieldwise_init struct Move[backend: ExecutionBackend](System): """Selects the execution target explicitly.""" def update(mut self, mut context: SystemContext[...]) raises: """Runs one synchronous movement step. Args: context: Access to this simulation's world. Raises: Error: If resource preparation or execution fails. """ context.run[move, backend=Self.backend]() Move[ExecutionBackend.DEVICE]() runs move on the device, and Move[ExecutionBackend.HOST]() runs it sequentially on the host, which is also the default when you omit backend. The ExecutionBackend value is a compile-time parameter, so each system specialization runs on one target.

data.separate-the-owner-from-the-kernel-view#:
The particle types follow the portable particle model, and Time stays an ordinary read-only resource.
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle coordinates, using device-compatible scalar arithmetic.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle displacement per unit of simulated time.""" var dx: Float32 var dy: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Time(Copyable, Movable): """Shared simulated step length.""" var delta: Float32 The stage adds two resource sources, the owning values stored in the world:
comptime Samples = BufferResource[DType.float32] @fieldwise_init struct Grid(DevicePassableResource): """Owns samples and dimensions on the host.""" comptime Adapter = GridAdapter var values: List[Float32] var width: Int var height: Int @fieldwise_init struct GridView(TrivialRegisterPassable): """Borrows immutable samples in the current target's address space.""" var values: Pointer[Float32, ImmUntrackedOrigin] var width: Int var height: Int def last(self) -> Float32: """Reads the final sample of a validated nonempty grid. Returns: The bottom-right sample. """ return self.values[unsafe_offset=self.width * self.height - 1] Samples is a BufferResource, the built-in source for a contiguous buffer of scalars. Its kernel view is ReadBuffer, which has a length and bounds-checked indexing. Grid is a custom DevicePassableResource: it owns a List and names the adapter that creates its view, GridView. GridView holds a pointer to the samples and the grid’s dimensions, and last() reads the final sample. The pointer is target-local: it refers to host memory in a host run and to device memory in a device run.

