Read, change, add, remove, and replace the components of one entity through its handle. Use these operations in setup code, in tests, or in a system that handles a few known entities. To change every entity that matches a filter, use a kernel or a batch operation instead.
The examples use the particle components from Your first simulation and one more component, Frozen, which replaces Velocity when a particle stops:
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable): """Where a particle is.""" var x: Float64 """Horizontal coordinate.""" var y: Float64 """Vertical coordinate.""" @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable): """How far a particle moves per unit of time.""" var dx: Float64 """Horizontal speed.""" var dy: Float64 """Vertical speed.""" @fieldwise_init struct Frozen(Copyable, Movable): """Marks a particle that stopped and remembers its last speed.""" var speed: Float64 """Speed before the particle froze.""" var world = World[Position, Velocity, Frozen]() var walker = world.storage.add_entity(Position(0, 0), Velocity(1, 0)) var marker = world.storage.add_entity(Position(3, 3)) walker has a position and a velocity; marker has only a position. Inside a system, call the same methods on context.world[].storage.

data.classify-particles-by-grid-cell#Classify:
The stage uses the portable particle model and an Int32 marker for particles that should not be grouped.
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle coordinates, using device-compatible scalar arithmetic.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle displacement per unit of simulated time.""" var dx: Float32 var dy: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Time(Copyable, Movable): """Shared simulated step length.""" var delta: Float32 A classifier computes a cluster key for each entity: a UInt64 that names its group. Entities with equal keys belong to the same cluster. This classifier uses one cluster per grid cell:
comptime spatial_filter = Filter().read[Position]().exclude[Int32]() @fieldwise_init struct Grid(SpatialClassifier): """Classifies eligible particles by a bounded planar Morton cell.""" comptime Accessor = EntityAccessor[spatial_filter] var cell_size: Float64 def classify(self, entity: Self.Accessor) raises -> UInt64: """Reads only the declared position input. Args: entity: Borrowed classifier inputs; never retained. Raises: Error: If the cell width, coordinates, or cell bounds are invalid. Returns: The exact cell's Morton key. """ ref position = entity.get[Position]() return morton_key_3d( grid_cell(Float64(position.x), self.cell_size), grid_cell(Float64(position.y), self.cell_size), ) spatial_filter decides which entities Larecs classifies: those with a Position and no Int32 marker. Grid implements SpatialClassifier; its Accessor must use exactly that filter, with read-only access. grid_cell rounds a coordinate down to its cell, so -0.1 with cell width 1 lies in cell -1. morton_key_3d combines the cell coordinates into one key; the planar simulation leaves z at 0. Both raise for coordinates outside their documented bounds instead of clamping.

